---
title: 'Singapore’s New Energy Plan: Why the Home Is Now Part of the Solution'
date: 2026-10-27
---
Singapore has just announced its most significant energy infrastructure build-up in decades. Peak electricity demand is set to climb from 8.6 GW today to as high as 13 GW by 2031, driven by semiconductors, data centres and the electrification of transport. The government is responding with five new natural-gas plants, a S$5.3 billion grid upgrade, a higher solar target of 4 GWp by 2035, more energy storage, and low-carbon electricity imports.

This is not just an industrial story. When national demand rises this fast and supply remains concentrated (natural gas still provides about 95 per cent of our electricity), every household feels the pressure through price, reliability and the practical limits of what the grid can absorb. The plan strengthens the supply side. The other half of the equation is how homes use, shift and conserve energy.

### Energy security is no longer only a national project

Minister Tan See Leng has been clear: Singapore cannot fully insulate itself from regional shocks, whether gas-supply interruptions or disruptions further afield. The response is diversification—more LNG capacity, hydrogen-ready plants, solar, storage and imports from multiple countries. Households and businesses are assured that energy security remains a top priority and that decarbonisation will not come at the expense of livelihoods.

For a home with solar, that security has a second layer. The more of your own generation you can use on site, the less you depend on the grid at the moments when it is most stressed or most expensive. Energy security at the household level is the ability to align what you generate with what you actually need.

### Rising demand meets a supply-side reality

The projected jump in peak demand is large, and the timing depends on how quickly new industrial and transport loads come online. Even with five new gas plants adding roughly 26 per cent more generation capacity, the system will need greater flexibility. Storage is being scaled from 350 MWh today to over 1 GWh by 2030 precisely so the grid can absorb variability and respond to disruptions.

At home, the same dynamic plays out at smaller scale. Solar production peaks in the middle of the day. Household demand often peaks in the evening. Without active management, surplus solar is exported at a lower value while the home buys power back later. That mismatch is the residential version of the broader supply-demand tension the national plan is trying to manage.

### Sustainability has a ceiling—and a floor

Solar remains Singapore’s most viable indigenous renewable source, yet even the raised 4 GWp target is expected to cover no more than about 10 per cent of projected electricity demand in 2050. Imports of low-carbon electricity and a more connected regional grid are therefore essential. Sustainability at national scale will always be a mix of domestic generation, imports and efficiency.

For individual homes, the practical contribution is maximising the solar that is already on the roof. Every kilowatt-hour used on site instead of exported and re-imported later reduces both the household’s grid draw and the pressure on the wider system. Conservation is not the opposite of growth; it is the part of the energy equation that does not require a new power plant.

### Reduce and conserve—on your terms

The national plan focuses on building capacity and flexibility. Households can act on the other side of the meter: shifting flexible loads into solar hours, avoiding unnecessary evening peaks, and making small, data-driven changes that compound over a month. These actions do not require lifestyle sacrifice. They require knowing, in real time, what the home is actually generating and using.

That is the gap most households still face. Generation sits in one app, consumption in another, and the bill arrives later with no clear link between the two. Without that link, conservation stays theoretical.

### What Equilux AI does with this reality

Equilux AI consolidates your home’s energy data—solar production, consumption and the grid interaction between them—and continuously optimises around how you actually live. It does not ask you to become a grid operator. It recommends specific, practical actions based on your home’s real patterns: when to run the air-conditioner or charge the EV so that midday solar covers more of the load, which habits are quietly raising the evening bill, and where a small shift delivers a measurable monthly saving.

As Singapore builds the next generation of energy infrastructure, the homes that treat generation and consumption as one system will feel the least impact from rising demand and supply constraints. The infrastructure plan secures the system. Equilux helps the household secure its own share of that system—lower cost, higher self-consumption, and less dependence on the moments when the grid is most stretched.

If you have solar and want the data from your own home to drive the next saving, Equilux AI is built for exactly that.
